<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>A Linear Function and a Quadratic Function</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02531</md:content-id>
    <md:title>A Linear Function and a Quadratic Function</md:title>
    <md:uuid>0f174cdb-6dd6-43dc-9485-e57a37bb5b34</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (10 minutes)</title>
<para>The purpose of this activity is for students to use what they know to solve an unfamiliar problem. This is a preview of solving a system consisting of a linear equation and a quadratic equation algebraically and graphically, which students will study more in depth in a future course.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para> Here are graphs of a linear function and a quadratic function. The quadratic function is defined by the expression \((x−4)^2−5\).</para>
<para>Find the coordinates of \(P\), \(Q\), and \(R\) without using graphing technology. Show your reasoning.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1c1813e4c9101d3e2aa096dcc45915999fb4534a"/></media></figure></para>
<title>Student Response</title>
<para>\(P=(0,11)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> The quadratic expression \((x−4)^2−5\) can be rewritten in standard form as \(x^2−8x+11\). The constant term 11 means the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,11)\). </item>
</list>
<para> \(Q=(3,-4)\) and \(R=(6,-1)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item>The line has a \(y\)-intercept of \((0,-7)\) and a slope of 1, so its equation is \(y=x−7\). \(Q\) and \(R\) are the points where the two functions have the same values, so \(x^2−8x+11=x−7\) at those points. Solving the equation gives \(x=6\) and \(x=3\). </item>
</list>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}\;x^2-8x+11\;&amp;=&amp;x-7 \\ x^2-8x-x+11+7\;&amp;=&amp;0 \\ x^2-9x+18\;&amp;=&amp;0 \\ (x-6)(x-3)\;&amp;=&amp;0 \end{array}\)</para>
<para class="os-raise-noindent">\( x-6=0\) or \(x-3=0\)  \(\;\;\;\;\;\;x=6\) or \(x=3\)</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Invite one or more students to describe how they found their solutions in their own words, or if they got stuck, demonstrate the progress that they made.</para></section>
</content>
